<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2" id="process_extractspikes_sdiff">

	<refmeta>
		<refentrytitle>process_extractspikes_sdiff</refentrytitle>
		<manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
	</refmeta>

	<refnamediv>
		<refname>process_extractspikes_sdiff</refname>
		<refpurpose>detect and extract spike waveforms using the spatial derivative across probe channels to suppress common-mode noise before threshold crossing.</refpurpose>
	</refnamediv>

	<refsynopsisdiv>
		<cmdsynopsis>
		<command>process_extractspikes_sdiff</command>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-n</option> <replaceable>nChannels</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-c</option> <replaceable>channels</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-w</option> <replaceable>waveformLength</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-p</option> <replaceable>peakSample</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-r</option> <replaceable>refractoryPeriod</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-f</option> <replaceable>thresholdFactor</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<option>-Z</option> <replaceable>sizeBytes</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg>
			<option>-B</option> <replaceable>startByte</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg>
			<option>-l</option> <replaceable>peakSearchLength</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg>
			<option>-d</option> <replaceable>sdiffOrder</replaceable>
		</arg>
		<arg>
			<option>-v</option>
		</arg>
		<arg choice="plain">
			<replaceable>basename</replaceable>
		</arg>
		</cmdsynopsis>
	</refsynopsisdiv>

	<refsect1><title>Description</title>

		<para>
			<command>process_extractspikes_sdiff</command> is a drop-in companion to
			<command>process_extractspikes</command> that applies a spatial derivative
			across the ordered channels of each electrode group before evaluating
			threshold crossings. Because genuine spike currents are spatially
			localised while common-mode artefacts (motion noise, electrical
			interference) are correlated across all contacts, the derivative
			enhances single-unit signals relative to noise and suppresses
			artefacts that would otherwise trigger false detections.
		</para>

		<para>
			Detection (threshold crossing and peak search) operates entirely on
			the spatial-derivative signal. Waveform extraction writes the
			<emphasis>original, un-differentiated</emphasis> data to the
			<filename>.spk</filename> output files so that downstream PCA and
			clustering tools see unmodified raw amplitudes, exactly as they would
			with <command>process_extractspikes</command>.
		</para>

		<para>
			The detection threshold is computed internally using the Quiroga
			(2004) robust noise estimator applied to the derivative signal:
			<emphasis>threshold = factor × 4σ</emphasis>,
			where <emphasis>σ = median(|sdiff|) / 0.6745</emphasis>.
			No external call to <command>process_medianthreshold</command> is
			required.
		</para>

		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-n</option> <replaceable>nChannels</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Total number of channels in the file.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-c</option> <replaceable>channels</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Grouped channel list (e.g. <literal>0,1,2:4,5,7</literal> for
						2 groups with 3 channels each). Channels within each group
						<emphasis>must</emphasis> be listed in physical probe order
						(tip-to-base) so that spatial neighbours are adjacent.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-w</option> <replaceable>waveformLength</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Number of samples per extracted waveform window.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-p</option> <replaceable>peakSample</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Position of the peak within the waveform (1-based, in samples).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-r</option> <replaceable>refractoryPeriod</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Minimum number of samples between two consecutive spikes
						(refractory period).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-f</option> <replaceable>thresholdFactor</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Threshold multiplier applied to the Quiroga noise estimate of
						the spatial-derivative signal:
						<emphasis>threshold = factor × 4σ</emphasis>.
						Typical values: 3.0 – 5.0.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-Z</option> <replaceable>sizeBytes</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Length in bytes of the noise-estimation window within the
						<filename>.fil</filename> file.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-B</option> <replaceable>startByte</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Byte offset into the <filename>.fil</filename> file at which the
						noise-estimation window begins (default 0).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-l</option> <replaceable>peakSearchLength</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Size of the window within which to look for the peak after a
						threshold crossing (in samples). Defaults to
						<replaceable>waveformLength</replaceable>. Do not change this
						unless you know what you are doing.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-d</option> <replaceable>sdiffOrder</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Spatial derivative order:
						<literal>0</literal> — none (identical to
						<command>process_extractspikes</command>);
						<literal>1</literal> — first-order difference
						(<emphasis>s[i] = x[i] − x[i+1]</emphasis>);
						<literal>2</literal> — discrete Laplacian (default,
						<emphasis>s[i] = x[i] − ½(x[i−1] + x[i+1])</emphasis>,
						edge channels use one-sided differences).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>-v</option></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Verbose mode.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>basename</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Common basename (filename without extension) for the input
						<filename>.fil</filename> file and the output
						<filename>.res</filename> / <filename>.spk</filename> files.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>

	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Output files</title>
		<para>
			For each electrode group <emphasis>N</emphasis>, the program writes:
		</para>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>basename.res.N</filename></term>
				<listitem><para>Spike timestamps (binary int64, one per spike).</para></listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>basename.spk.N</filename></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Raw waveform snippets (binary int16, interleaved by channel
						then by sample). The waveforms are taken from the
						<emphasis>original</emphasis> signal, not the spatial derivative.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Environment</title>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><envar>PROCESS_EXTRACTSPIKES_MEM_CAP_GB</envar></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Maximum in-memory waveform buffer per group, in GB
						(default 2). Groups whose waveform data would exceed this
						limit are written to disk in streaming mode.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>See Also</title>
		<para>
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_extractspikes_sdiff</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>process_extractspikes</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>process_medianthreshold</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>
		</para>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Author</title>
		<para>
		<author>
			<firstname>Gravios</firstname>
			<surname>NeuroSuite-3 contributors</surname>
			<contrib>Original author</contrib>
		</author>
		</para>
	</refsect1>

</refentry>
